course: geography
lesson: 4
title: Time zones and the date line
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, 15 degrees an hour, so noon slides westwards around the planet like a spotlight.
explain: In 1337, when EU5 starts, every town simply kept its own sun time, and nobody minded because no horse or ship was fast enough for a few minutes to matter.
explain: Trains changed that: a timetable needs one clock, so British railways took London time in the 1840s, and the world later split into time zones counted from UTC.
explain: Somewhere the days must change, and that seam is the International Date Line in the Pacific: cross it westwards and you jump a whole day ahead.
explain: Magellan's crew, brought home by Elcano in 1522, found their log one day behind the date in Spain, because they had sailed westwards round the world with the Sun.
fact: The Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, so it turns 15 degrees of longitude every hour, which is the basic width of a time zone; one degree takes 4 minutes, so a town 2 or 3 degrees west of another sees noon about 10 minutes later.
fact: The world sets its clocks from Coordinated Universal Time, or UTC, and the UK uses Greenwich Mean Time (equal to UTC) in winter and British Summer Time, one hour ahead, in summer.
fact: Before railways each town kept its own local time from the Sun, and British railways began using London time in the 1840s, with the law following in 1880.
fact: The 48 neighbouring US states use four main time zones, Eastern (with New York), Central, Mountain and Pacific (with Los Angeles), each one hour behind the last, so Los Angeles is 3 hours behind New York, and Alaska and Hawaii have their own.
fact: China uses a single time zone, UTC+8, for the whole country, even though it is about as wide as five standard zones; the clock follows Beijing in the east, so in the far west the Sun rises very late by the clock.
fact: Not all time zones differ by whole hours, and India uses UTC+5:30 while Nepal uses UTC+5:45.
fact: The International Date Line runs mostly along 180 degrees longitude through the Pacific, zigzagging round island groups, and crossing it westwards you jump forward one calendar day.
fact: Kiribati moved the date line in 1995 so all its islands share the same date, and its Line Islands at UTC+14 are among the first places on Earth to start each new day.
fact: Samoa jumped to the west side of the date line by skipping Friday 30 December 2011 entirely, so it now has the same date as its big trading partners Australia and New Zealand instead of being a day behind.
fact: When the survivors of Magellan's expedition, led home by Juan Sebastián Elcano, returned to Spain in 1522, they found their log was one day behind the date on land.
fact: Many countries move their clocks forward an hour in summer, and the UK changes on the last Sunday in March and the last Sunday in October; near the equator days and nights stay about 12 hours long all year, so most countries there never change their clocks.
fact: France uses 12 time zones, more than any other country, if its overseas territories are counted.
fact: In the EU5 period there were no time zones at all; each place kept its own sun time.
exercise: How many degrees of longitude does the Earth turn in one hour? | 15 degrees | 24 degrees | 1 degree | 60 degrees
exercise: What is the basic width of one time zone? | 15 degrees of longitude | 24 degrees of longitude | 1 degree of latitude | 100 kilometres
exercise: What does UTC stand for? | Coordinated Universal Time | United Time of Countries | Universal Train Clock | Upper Time Circle
exercise: When it is 12:00 UTC in July, what time do clocks show in London? | 13:00, British Summer Time | 12:00, Greenwich Mean Time | 11:00 | 14:00
exercise: When did British railways begin using London time? | In the 1840s | In the 1640s | In the 1940s | In 1337
exercise: How many main time zones do the 48 neighbouring US states use? | Four | One | Six | Three
exercise: How many time zones does China use? | One, UTC+8 | Five, one for each region | Three | Four
exercise: When it is 12:00 UTC, what time is it in India? | 17:30 | 17:00 | 12:30 | 18:00
exercise: Which country uses UTC+5:45? | Nepal | India | China | Japan
exercise: Along which longitude does the date line mostly run? | 180 degrees | 0 degrees, at Greenwich | 90 degrees west | 15 degrees east
exercise: If you cross the date line flying west on Monday evening, what day is it when you land? | Tuesday | Monday | Sunday | Wednesday
exercise: Why did Kiribati move the date line in 1995? | So all its islands share the same date | To be the last place to see each day | To join the US time zones | To match London's time
exercise: Which day did Samoa skip in 2011? | Friday 30 December | Saturday 31 December | Monday 2 January 2012 | Thursday 29 December
exercise: When Elcano's crew got home in 1522, how was their log wrong? | It was one day behind | It was one day ahead of the date | It was one whole week behind | It was exactly right after all
exercise: When does the UK put its clocks forward? | The last Sunday in March | The first Sunday in January | The last Sunday in October | The first day of summer in June
exercise: Which country uses the most time zones, counting its overseas lands? | France, with 12 | Russia, with 11 | The United States, with 6 | China, with 5
exercise: What time system did towns use in 1337? | Each kept its own local sun time | They all used Greenwich time | They used UTC | Kings set one time for all of Europe
test: Local noon in two places is 3 hours apart. How far apart are they in longitude? | 45 degrees | 3 degrees | 15 degrees | 90 degrees
test: Why did trains, not ships or horses, force countries to share one clock time? | Trains were fast and needed one timetable for all stations | Horses could not tell the time | Ships always used London time | Trains ran only at noon
test: footy starts 7pm in New York, mate in LA wants to watch live. What time for him? | 4pm | 10pm | 7pm | 1pm
test: What is strange about sunrise in western China? | It comes very late by the clock, because of the single zone | It comes before midnight | The Sun rises in the west there | There is no sunrise in winter
test: If you sailed around the world eastwards, would you gain or lose a day on your log? | Your log would be a day ahead | Your log would be a day behind | Nothing would change | You would lose a week
test: Why would trade with Australia matter more to Samoa by 2011? | Being a day behind left only four shared working days a week | Samoa wanted New Year to come later | The United States closed its ports | Australia owned Samoa
test: Why do countries close to the equator not need daylight saving time? | Day length hardly changes there through the year | They have no clocks | It is always night there in the winter months | The Sun never sets at the equator
test: Why does France have more time zones than Russia, which is much bigger? | Its overseas lands are spread round the globe | France is wider than Russia | Paris uses several clocks | Russia uses only one zone
test: Kid says "the date line is straight cos it's just 180 degrees". Right? | No, it zigzags round island groups | Yes, it is perfectly straight from pole to pole | Yes, nobody lives near it | No, it runs through London
test: In EU5 your army marches from Lisbon to Moscow. Why would no real general of 1500 have worried about time zones? | Every town used its own sun time and travel was slow | Time zones were secret | Lisbon and Moscow had the same sun time | Armies carried UTC clocks
test: Bristol is west of London. Does local noon come before or after London's? | After, about 10 minutes later | Before, about 10 minutes earlier | At exactly the same moment | One hour later
exam: Ascertain the angular rotation of the Earth, expressed in degrees of longitude, accomplished in the course of one hour. | Fifteen degrees | Twenty-four degrees | One degree of longitude | Thirty degrees of longitude
exam: Whereas a traveller traverses the International Date Line in a westerly direction, determine the consequent alteration to the calendar date. | It advances by one day | It recedes by one day | It remains unaltered | It advances by one week
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a uniform national time was adopted by the British railways. | Railway timetables required a single common time | Local solar time had been abolished by Parliament | Ships required it for navigation | The Sun no longer kept regular time
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Republic of India's standard time. | It is five hours and thirty minutes ahead of UTC | It is five hours ahead of UTC | It is identical to UTC | It is eight hours ahead of UTC
